('q-exp-cv-001', 'clinical-vignette', 'CV', 'easy', '{"question":"A 62-year-old male with prior MI presents with palpitations and presyncope. ECG shows a wide-complex tachycardia at 160 bpm with AV dissociation and fusion beats. BP is 88/60 mmHg. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["A. Ventricular tachycardia","B. Supraventricular tachycardia with aberrant conduction","C. Atrial flutter with 2:1 block","D. Wolff-Parkinson-White with antidromic conduction"],"correctAnswer":"A","correctAnswerIndex":0,"explanation":"AV dissociation plus fusion beats in a wide-complex tachycardia is virtually pathognomonic for ventricular tachycardia (VT). A prior MI creates a re-entrant circuit in scar tissue — the most common cause of sustained monomorphic VT. SVT with aberrancy can mimic VT but would not produce AV dissociation or fusion beats. The hemodynamic instability (BP 88/60) further supports VT. Immediate synchronized cardioversion is indicated when the patient is unstable.","conditionName":"Ventricular Tachycardia","system":"CV","subcategory":"Arrhythmias","tags":["CV","VT","arrhythmia","AV-dissociation","post-MI"],"generatedWith":"expansion-v1"}',
'first', 'diagnosis', 'pending', NOW()),

('q-exp-cv-002', 'clinical-vignette', 'CV', 'medium', '{"question":"A 58-year-old male collapses at a grocery store. Bystanders initiate CPR. Paramedics arrive 4 minutes later and attach a defibrillator, which shows pulseless ventricular tachycardia at 200 bpm. What is the immediate next step?","options":["A. Unsynchronized defibrillation at 200 J (biphasic)","B. Synchronized cardioversion at 100 J","C. IV amiodarone 300 mg bolus","D. IV lidocaine 1.5 mg/kg bolus"],"correctAnswer":"A","correctAnswerIndex":0,"explanation":"Pulseless VT is a shockable rhythm requiring immediate unsynchronized defibrillation (defibrillation, not cardioversion, because the patient has no pulse and synchronization cannot be reliably performed). Biphasic 200 J is the standard initial energy. Synchronized cardioversion is used for unstable patients who still have a pulse. Amiodarone and lidocaine are adjuncts after the first shock, not the first intervention. CPR should resume immediately after each shock.","conditionName":"Pulseless Ventricular Tachycardia","system":"CV","subcategory":"Arrhythmias","tags":["CV","VT","pulseless","ACLS","defibrillation"],"generatedWith":"expansion-v1"}',
'second', 'management', 'pending', NOW()),

('q-exp-cv-003', 'clinical-vignette', 'CV', 'medium', '{"question":"A 74-year-old female presents with fatigue and dizziness. ECG shows P waves at 80 bpm and QRS complexes at 38 bpm with no relationship between them. The PR interval varies throughout the tracing. What is the diagnosis and most appropriate next step?","options":["A. Third-degree (complete) AV block — transvenous pacing followed by permanent pacemaker","B. Second-degree Mobitz I — observation and monitoring","C. Second-degree Mobitz II — IV atropine","D. Junctional tachycardia — IV metoprolol"],"correctAnswer":"A","correctAnswerIndex":0,"explanation":"Complete AV block (third-degree) is characterized by complete dissociation of atrial and ventricular activity: the atria fire at their intrinsic rate (80 bpm) and the ventricles escape at a slow idioventricular rate (38 bpm) with no fixed PR relationship. This is a hemodynamically significant bradyarrhythmia requiring urgent pacing — first transcutaneous or transvenous, then permanent pacemaker implantation. Atropine is rarely effective for infranodal (His-Purkinje) complete block and is a temporary bridge at best.","conditionName":"Complete AV Block","system":"CV","subcategory":"Arrhythmias","tags":["CV","AV-block","complete","pacemaker","bradycardia"],"generatedWith":"expansion-v1"}',
'second', 'diagnosis', 'pending', NOW()),

('q-exp-cv-004', 'clinical-vignette', 'CV', 'medium', '{"question":"A 68-year-old male with known coronary artery disease presents after two syncopal episodes. ECG shows intermittent non-conducted P waves with a constant PR interval before the dropped beat. QRS is wide with right bundle branch morphology. What is the most appropriate management?","options":["A. Permanent pacemaker implantation","B. Long-term oral metoprolol","C. IV atropine infusion","D. Observation with Holter monitoring"],"correctAnswer":"A","correctAnswerIndex":0,"explanation":"This describes Mobitz Type II second-degree AV block (constant PR interval with sudden dropped QRS), which occurs in the His-Purkinje system (infranodal). The wide QRS with RBBB morphology confirms infranodal disease. Mobitz II carries a high risk of progression to complete heart block and sudden death — permanent pacemaker is the definitive treatment. Atropine is ineffective for infranodal block. Mobitz I (Wenckebach) has a progressively lengthening PR interval and is generally benign, requiring observation only.","conditionName":"Mobitz Type II AV Block","system":"CV","subcategory":"Arrhythmias","tags":["CV","Mobitz-II","AV-block","pacemaker","syncope"],"generatedWith":"expansion-v1"}',
'second', 'management', 'pending', NOW()),

('q-exp-cv-005', 'clinical-vignette', 'CV', 'medium', '{"question":"A 55-year-old male smoker with hypertension and hyperlipidemia reports chest pressure with exertion that resolves at rest. He has no symptoms at rest and ECG is normal. Which diagnostic test is most appropriate as the initial evaluation for suspected stable angina?","options":["A. Exercise stress ECG (Bruce protocol)","B. Coronary CT angiography","C. Right heart catheterization","D. Resting echocardiogram"],"correctAnswer":"A","correctAnswerIndex":0,"explanation":"Exercise stress testing (exercise ECG) is the preferred initial test for patients with intermediate pre-test probability of CAD who can exercise and have an interpretable ECG. It provides functional information about ischemia, exercise capacity, and hemodynamic response. Coronary CTA is preferred when the pre-test probability is low-to-intermediate and noninvasive anatomic assessment is desired. Right heart catheterization evaluates pulmonary hypertension and heart failure hemodynamics, not ischemia. Resting echo will not detect exercise-induced ischemia.","conditionName":"Stable Angina","system":"CV","subcategory":"Ischemic Heart Disease","tags":["CV","stable-angina","stress-test","CAD","diagnosis"],"generatedWith":"expansion-v1"}',
'second', 'diagnostics', 'pending', NOW()),

('q-exp-cv-006', 'clinical-vignette', 'CV', 'medium', '{"question":"A 60-year-old female with stable angina is started on medical therapy. She has no contraindications. Which drug class is considered first-line for reducing anginal episodes and improving exercise tolerance?","options":["A. Beta-blockers","B. Calcium channel blockers (dihydropyridine)","C. Long-acting nitrates","D. Ranolazine"],"correctAnswer":"A","correctAnswerIndex":0,"explanation":"Beta-blockers are first-line for stable angina because they reduce myocardial oxygen demand by decreasing heart rate, contractility, and blood pressure — the three primary determinants of myocardial O2 consumption. They also reduce mortality in patients with prior MI. Calcium channel blockers (dihydropyridines like amlodipine) are second-line or used in combination. Long-acting nitrates are effective for symptom control but do not reduce mortality and require nitrate-free intervals to prevent tolerance. Ranolazine is used as add-on therapy.","conditionName":"Stable Angina Medical Management","system":"CV","subcategory":"Ischemic Heart Disease","tags":["CV","stable-angina","beta-blocker","management","pharmaceuticals"],"generatedWith":"expansion-v1"}',
'second', 'pharmaceutical', 'pending', NOW()),

('q-exp-cv-007', 'clinical-vignette', 'CV', 'medium', '{"question":"A 65-year-old male develops severe dyspnea 3 days after an inferior STEMI managed with thrombolytics. On auscultation you hear a new harsh holosystolic murmur at the apex radiating to the axilla. Pulmonary edema is present on chest X-ray. What is the most likely cause?","options":["A. Acute mitral regurgitation from papillary muscle rupture","B. Ventricular septal defect from septal necrosis","C. Aortic stenosis exacerbation","D. Aortic regurgitation from aortic root dissection"],"correctAnswer":"A","correctAnswerIndex":0,"explanation":"Papillary muscle rupture is a feared mechanical complication of inferior MI (posteromedial papillary muscle is supplied by the RCA, which is typically culprit in inferior MI). It presents 2–7 days post-MI with acute severe MR: harsh holosystolic murmur radiating to the axilla, acute pulmonary edema, and hemodynamic compromise. The VSD from septal rupture also produces a holosystolic murmur but is heard at the left lower sternal border with a step-up in oxygen saturation from RA to RV on right heart catheterization. Immediate surgical repair or mechanical circulatory support is required.","conditionName":"Acute Mitral Regurgitation - Papillary Muscle Rupture","system":"CV","subcategory":"Acute MI Complications","tags":["CV","MR","mitral-regurgitation","papillary-muscle","STEMI-complication"],"generatedWith":"expansion-v1"}',
'second', 'history_pe', 'pending', NOW()),

('q-exp-cv-008', 'clinical-vignette', 'CV', 'easy', '{"question":"Which patient population should undergo one-time screening abdominal ultrasound for abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA) according to the USPSTF?","options":["A. Men aged 65–75 who have ever smoked (≥100 cigarettes lifetime)","B. All men and women aged 65–75","C. Women aged 65–75 who have ever smoked","D. Men aged 55–75 with a family history of AAA"],"correctAnswer":"A","correctAnswerIndex":0,"explanation":"The USPSTF recommends one-time screening abdominal ultrasound for AAA specifically in men aged 65–75 who have ever smoked (defined as ≥100 cigarettes in a lifetime). The evidence does not support routine screening in women who smoked or in asymptomatic non-smokers, as the absolute benefit is lower and the harms (unnecessary procedures) outweigh benefits in those populations. Women who have never smoked have a low prevalence of AAA. This is a Grade B recommendation.","conditionName":"Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm Screening","system":"CV","subcategory":"Aortic Disease","tags":["CV","AAA","screening","USPSTF","health-maintenance"],"generatedWith":"expansion-v1"}',
'first', 'health_maintenance', 'pending', NOW()),

('q-exp-cv-009', 'clinical-vignette', 'CV', 'medium', '{"question":"A 70-year-old male with known 6.5 cm infrarenal AAA presents to the ED with sudden severe tearing abdominal pain radiating to the back and hypotension (BP 80/50). Abdomen is pulsatile and tender. What is the most appropriate immediate management?","options":["A. Emergent surgical repair (open or endovascular)","B. CT angiography of the abdomen and pelvis","C. IV heparin anticoagulation","D. Permissive hypotension + IV nitroprusside to reduce aortic wall stress"],"correctAnswer":"A","correctAnswerIndex":0,"explanation":"The clinical presentation is classic for ruptured AAA: known large AAA, sudden severe tearing abdominal/back pain, pulsatile abdominal mass, and hemodynamic instability. This is a surgical emergency with mortality approaching 90% if untreated. The patient should be taken directly to the OR for emergent open or endovascular repair (EVAR). CT angiography is appropriate only if the patient is hemodynamically stable enough to tolerate imaging (some institutions use it to plan EVAR). IV heparin is contraindicated in rupture. Nitroprusside is used in aortic dissection, not rupture.","conditionName":"Ruptured Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm","system":"CV","subcategory":"Aortic Disease","tags":["CV","AAA","rupture","vascular-emergency","surgical-repair"],"generatedWith":"expansion-v1"}',
'second', 'management', 'pending', NOW()),

('q-exp-cv-010', 'clinical-vignette', 'CV', 'medium', '{"question":"A 24-year-old male athlete presents with palpitations during exercise. Resting ECG shows a short PR interval (0.10 sec), a slurred upstroke of the QRS (delta wave), and a wide QRS complex. He has no structural heart disease. What is the primary concern with this arrhythmia syndrome?","options":["A. Development of atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular conduction via the accessory pathway","B. Progression to complete heart block","C. Degeneration into ventricular fibrillation from a prolonged QT interval","D. Embolic stroke from intracardiac thrombus"],"correctAnswer":"A","correctAnswerIndex":0,"explanation":"Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) syndrome features an accessory atrioventricular pathway (Bundle of Kent) producing a delta wave, short PR, and wide QRS at rest. The primary lethal risk is atrial fibrillation with rapid conduction through the accessory pathway (which lacks decremental conduction), producing extremely fast ventricular rates (>300 bpm) that can degenerate into ventricular fibrillation and sudden cardiac death. AV nodal blocking agents (adenosine, digoxin, beta-blockers, CCBs) are contraindicated in WPW-AF as they preferentially block the AV node and allow even faster conduction via the accessory pathway.","conditionName":"Wolff-Parkinson-White Syndrome","system":"CV","subcategory":"Arrhythmias","tags":["CV","WPW","accessory-pathway","pre-excitation","delta-wave"],"generatedWith":"expansion-v1"}',
'second', 'diagnostics', 'pending', NOW()),

('q-exp-cv-011', 'clinical-vignette', 'CV', 'hard', '{"question":"A 28-year-old male with known WPW syndrome presents to the ED with a rapid irregular wide-complex tachycardia at 240 bpm. He is conscious with BP 100/70. ECG shows irregular RR intervals with varying QRS morphology. Which drug is most appropriate?","options":["A. IV procainamide","B. IV adenosine","C. IV diltiazem","D. IV digoxin"],"correctAnswer":"A","correctAnswerIndex":0,"explanation":"This presentation is pre-excited atrial fibrillation (AF with WPW) — irregular, wide-complex tachycardia due to rapid antegrade conduction over the accessory pathway. Procainamide blocks conduction in both the AV node and the accessory pathway, slowing ventricular response. Ibutilide and electrical cardioversion are alternatives. AV nodal blocking agents (adenosine, diltiazem, verapamil, beta-blockers, digoxin) are CONTRAINDICATED: by blocking the AV node, they accelerate conduction over the accessory pathway, which can precipitate ventricular fibrillation and sudden death. If hemodynamically unstable, immediate synchronized cardioversion is first-line.","conditionName":"WPW with Pre-excited Atrial Fibrillation","system":"CV","subcategory":"Arrhythmias","tags":["CV","WPW","AF","pre-excited","procainamide","antiarrhythmic"],"generatedWith":"expansion-v1"}',
'third', 'pharmaceutical', 'pending', NOW()),

('q-exp-cv-012', 'clinical-vignette', 'CV', 'medium', '{"question":"A 38-year-old female with limited systemic sclerosis presents with progressive dyspnea on exertion and near-syncope. Exam shows a loud P2, right ventricular heave, and peripheral edema. Echo shows RV dilation and estimated RVSP of 65 mmHg. Right heart catheterization confirms mean PAP 42 mmHg with PCWP 10 mmHg. What is the diagnosis?","options":["A. Pulmonary arterial hypertension (Group 1)","B. Pulmonary hypertension due to left heart disease (Group 2)","C. Pulmonary hypertension due to chronic lung disease (Group 3)","D. Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension (Group 4)"],"correctAnswer":"A","correctAnswerIndex":0,"explanation":"Pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH, Group 1) is defined by mean PAP ≥25 mmHg at rest with PCWP ≤15 mmHg (indicating the elevated pressures are not from left heart disease) and PVR >3 Wood units on right heart catheterization. Systemic sclerosis (scleroderma) is a major cause of PAH (connective tissue disease-associated PAH). Group 2 (left heart disease) would show elevated PCWP. Group 3 (lung disease) would be associated with COPD/ILD. Group 4 (CTEPH) is treated with pulmonary endarterectomy and would show evidence of organized thrombus on V/Q scan.","conditionName":"Pulmonary Arterial Hypertension","system":"CV","subcategory":"Pulmonary Hypertension","tags":["CV","PAH","pulmonary-hypertension","scleroderma","right-heart-cath"],"generatedWith":"expansion-v1"}',
'second', 'diagnosis', 'pending', NOW()),

('q-exp-cv-013', 'clinical-vignette', 'CV', 'medium', '{"question":"A 32-year-old female presents to the ED with sudden onset palpitations and mild dyspnea. ECG shows a regular narrow-complex tachycardia at 180 bpm with no visible P waves (or P waves within/immediately after the QRS). BP is 115/75. She has no structural heart disease. What is the first-line pharmacologic treatment?","options":["A. IV adenosine 6 mg rapid push","B. IV diltiazem 0.25 mg/kg","C. IV metoprolol 5 mg","D. IV amiodarone 150 mg over 10 minutes"],"correctAnswer":"A","correctAnswerIndex":0,"explanation":"This is AVNRT (atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia) — the most common cause of paroxysmal SVT in young women. Adenosine is first-line: given as a rapid IV push (6 mg, then 12 mg if unsuccessful), it transiently blocks AV nodal conduction and terminates the reentrant circuit. Vagal maneuvers (Valsalva, carotid sinus massage) should be attempted before adenosine. Diltiazem and metoprolol are alternatives but slower-acting. Amiodarone is reserved for refractory or recurrent SVT. DC cardioversion is used for hemodynamically unstable patients.","conditionName":"Supraventricular Tachycardia - AVNRT","system":"CV","subcategory":"Arrhythmias","tags":["CV","SVT","AVNRT","adenosine","narrow-complex"],"generatedWith":"expansion-v1"}',
'second', 'pharmaceutical', 'pending', NOW()),

('q-exp-cv-014', 'clinical-vignette', 'CV', 'medium', '{"question":"A 10-year-old male presents with fever, migratory polyarthritis, and a new heart murmur 3 weeks after a strep throat infection. Exam shows a friction rub and subcutaneous nodules. ECG shows a prolonged PR interval. What is the diagnosis, and which criteria are used to confirm it?","options":["A. Acute rheumatic fever — Jones criteria","B. Viral myocarditis — Dallas criteria","C. Infective endocarditis — modified Duke criteria","D. Juvenile idiopathic arthritis — ILAR criteria"],"correctAnswer":"A","correctAnswerIndex":0,"explanation":"Acute rheumatic fever (ARF) is a post-streptococcal inflammatory disease diagnosed using Jones criteria (revised 2015). Major criteria: carditis (new murmur, pericarditis, CHF), polyarthritis, Sydenham chorea, erythema marginatum, subcutaneous nodules. Minor criteria: fever, elevated ESR/CRP, prolonged PR interval. Diagnosis requires 2 major OR 1 major + 2 minor criteria, plus evidence of preceding GAS infection (elevated ASO titer, positive throat culture). The most feared complication is rheumatic heart disease (chronic mitral stenosis). Penicillin prophylaxis is required to prevent recurrence.","conditionName":"Acute Rheumatic Fever","system":"CV","subcategory":"Valvular and Inflammatory","tags":["CV","rheumatic-fever","Jones-criteria","streptococcus","carditis"],"generatedWith":"expansion-v1"}',
'second', 'diagnosis', 'pending', NOW()),

('q-exp-cv-015', 'clinical-vignette', 'CV', 'hard', '{"question":"A 72-year-old female with hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia develops sudden palpitations. ECG shows polymorphic ventricular tachycardia with a characteristic twisting of QRS axis around the isoelectric line and a prolonged QTc (520 ms) on baseline rhythm strips. What is the most appropriate immediate treatment?","options":["A. IV magnesium sulfate 2 g over 1–2 minutes","B. Synchronized cardioversion at 200 J","C. IV amiodarone 300 mg bolus","D. IV lidocaine 1.5 mg/kg"],"correctAnswer":"A","correctAnswerIndex":0,"explanation":"Torsades de pointes (TdP) is a polymorphic VT associated with a prolonged QT interval, characterized by the twisting QRS axis. The most critical immediate treatment is IV magnesium sulfate 2 g given rapidly, regardless of serum magnesium level — it stabilizes the cardiac membrane and shortens the action potential. Underlying reversible causes must also be corrected (hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, offending drugs). Amiodarone is CONTRAINDICATED as it further prolongs the QT interval and can worsen TdP. If pulseless, unsynchronized defibrillation is required. Overdrive pacing (70–90 bpm) is definitive therapy for recurrent TdP.","conditionName":"Torsades de Pointes","system":"CV","subcategory":"Arrhythmias","tags":["CV","TdP","torsades","long-QT","magnesium","polymorphic-VT"],"generatedWith":"expansion-v1"}',
'third', 'pharmaceutical', 'pending', NOW()),

